<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <!--
    Nop.Plugin.Payments.PayPalDirect - task 12.3
    (Requirements 5.5, 1.1-1.5, 2.4, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4, 5.1, 5.3, 3.1).

    THE PLUGIN RECIPE, applied verbatim - Sdk.Razor + AddRazorSupportForMvc, the Content/Link
    block, Private="false" PLUS the NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies target, and OutputPath
    PLUS AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath=false. Full reasoning: the annotated
    Nop.Plugin.DiscountRules.CustomerRoles.csproj (task 10.1), runtime-deferrals.md 83-86, and
    the group-12 restatement in Nop.Plugin.Payments.CheckMoneyOrder.csproj (task 12.1).

    ===================================================================================
    THIS IS THE ONE PLUGIN IN THE SOLUTION THAT DEPLOYS A PRIVATE THIRD-PARTY ASSEMBLY
    ===================================================================================
    3.90's project file carried the single <Private>True</Private> in all 20 legacy plugin
    projects: the PayPal REST SDK, PayPal 1.8.0. Task 10.1 scoped
    NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies to the "Nop." prefix precisely so this case keeps
    working, and it does - PayPal.dll lands in Plugins\Payments.PayPalDirect\ next to the
    plugin assembly, which is where PluginManager's shadow copy picks it up. Verified by
    listing the deployment directory, and asserted at runtime by
    Nop.Web.SmokeTests.PluginViewRenderTests.

    ===================================================================================
    THE PACKAGE HAS NO net10.0 VERSION, AND THERE IS NO SUCCESSOR. WHAT WAS DONE, AND WHY.
    ===================================================================================
    The `PayPal` package is DEPRECATED on nuget.org ("legacy and no longer maintained") and
    NO release of it publishes a netstandard or net(core) asset - 1.8.0, the final 1.9.1 and
    everything between ship lib/net40 + lib/net45 + lib/net451 only. PayPal's REST API v1
    .NET SDK was discontinued; the v2 SDK is a different product with a different API surface.
    So there is no version to advance to and no package to substitute.

    DECISION: pin 1.8.0 - the version 3.90 shipped - as a version-less PackageReference under
    central package management (src/Directory.Packages.props), consume its lib/net451 asset,
    and LEAVE THE RESULTING NU1701 WARNING VISIBLE. That follows the two precedents this
    migration has set: pinning an old package with a recorded reason (EPPlus 4.5.3.3,
    FluentValidation 7.6.105, NUnit 3.14.0, iTextSharp.LGPLv2.Core, SixLabors.ImageSharp
    2.1.13) and leaving an honest warning rather than suppressing it (the four remaining
    SYSLIB0014 WebRequest sites). The alternative - rewriting PayPalDirect's ~1000 lines of
    payment, recurring-payment, capture, refund, void and webhook logic against raw HTTP - is
    a rewrite of a money path with no test coverage available, which is precisely what the
    faithfulness rule forbids.

    *** AND IT WAS VERIFIED TO RUN, NOT ASSUMED TO. *** A net451 assembly referenced from
    net10.0 compiles happily and can still fail at the first call, so PayPal.dll's metadata
    was read (System.Reflection.Metadata) and its IL scanned for members belonging to
    assemblies that do not exist on .NET. Two were found:

      System.Web       -> System.Web.HttpUtility.UrlEncode / .ParseQueryString, reached from
                          PayPal.Util.SDKUtil.FormatURIPath - i.e. from EVERY resource GET the
                          plugin performs (Sale.Get, Authorization.Get, Capture.Get,
                          Agreement.Get, Webhook.Get) - plus Tokeninfo.CreateFromRefreshToken
                          and PayPalRelationalObject.GetTokenFromApprovalUrl.
      System.Configuration -> ConfigurationManager.GetSection / .AppSettings and the
                          ConfigurationSection/Element/Collection base types, reached from
                          PayPal.Api.ConfigManager's constructor and PayPal.Log.LogConfiguration.

    Both were then EXERCISED on net10.0 (throwaway console probe, deleted afterwards):

      * `System.Web` RESOLVES - the net10.0 shared framework ships a type-forwarding facade
        whose System.Web.HttpUtility lives in System.Web.HttpUtility.dll. SDKUtil.FormatURIPath
        returned the correct path. Nothing to do.
      * `System.Configuration` also resolves as a facade, but its targets live in the
        OUT-OF-BAND System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager package. Without that package
        present, `new OAuthTokenCredential(config)` - the FIRST thing
        PaypalHelper.GetApiContext does - threw
        `FileNotFoundException: Could not load file or assembly
        'System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager'`. With it present, every probe assertion
        passed. So the plugin would have compiled, deployed, been discovered, loaded, listed in
        the admin UI, and then failed on its first PayPal call with a missing-assembly error
        that no compiler warning predicts.

    That is why System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager is declared below. It is declared
    ExcludeAssets="runtime" because the HOST already carries it (Nop.Web resolves 9.0.11
    transitively through Microsoft.Data.SqlClient -> Azure.Identity, confirmed in
    Nop.Web.deps.json), and PluginManager loads plugin assemblies into the DEFAULT
    AssemblyLoadContext, which resolves their dependencies from the host's trusted-platform
    list. Deploying a second copy next to the plugin would instead be shadow-copied and loaded
    by PluginManager.PerformFileDeploy as the process's copy - the failure mode
    NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies exists to prevent for Nop.* assemblies. Deferral
    12.3-2 records that the host's copy arrives TRANSITIVELY, i.e. a change to the data
    provider's dependency graph would silently break this payment plugin, and the smoke tests
    now assert the type is loadable in the host so that regression is caught.

    Deleted in the classic -> SDK conversion: packages.config, app.config, web.config
    (transcribed to Views/_ViewImports.cshtml), the <Compile Include> list, the MVC 5
    <Reference>s into ..\..\packages\, the BCL <Reference>s, the Microsoft.CSharp.targets and
    Microsoft.Bcl.Build.targets imports plus the EnsureNuGetPackageBuildImports guard.
    Kept: Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AddRazorSupportForMvc>true</AddRazorSupportForMvc>
    <StaticWebAssetsEnabled>false</StaticWebAssetsEnabled>
    <OutputPath>..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web\Plugins\Payments.PayPalDirect\</OutputPath>
    <AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>false</AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The PayPal SDK. This ONE reference deliberately keeps its runtime asset, reproducing
      3.90's <Private>True</Private>: PayPal.dll is not carried by the host and the plugin
      cannot work without it. NU1701 is expected and left visible - see the note above.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="PayPal" />

    <!--
      3.90 declared both of these as <Reference> items with Private=False, i.e. compile
      against them, do not copy. ExcludeAssets="runtime" is the PackageReference spelling of
      that: the host already carries FluentValidation.dll and Newtonsoft.Json.dll, and a
      private copy next to the plugin would give the process a second one.

      Newtonsoft.Json must stay DECLARED even though nothing here would otherwise need it:
      PayPal 1.8.0's net451 dependency group asks for >= 7.0.1 and NuGet would resolve exactly
      7.0.1, which carries NU1903 (GHSA-5crp-9r3c-p9vr, HIGH severity). The central 13.0.4 pin
      lifts it. Measured both ways.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />

    <!-- See the long note above: PayPal.dll's System.Configuration facade forwards here. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Libraries\Nop.Core\Nop.Core.csproj" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Libraries\Nop.Services\Nop.Services.csproj" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Presentation\Nop.Web.Framework\Nop.Web.Framework.csproj" Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Filters on the "Nop." prefix, so PayPal.dll is untouched. That scoping was task 10.1's
    deliberate choice for exactly this plugin.
  -->
  <Target Name="NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies" AfterTargets="ResolveReferences">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ReferenceCopyLocalPaths
          Remove="@(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths)"
          Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Filename)').StartsWith('Nop.'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================
    DEFECT FOUND BY EXECUTION AT TASK 12.3 - THE RECIPE ALONE DOES NOT DEPLOY PayPal.dll
    ===================================================================================
    Task 10.1's template asserts that NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies "filters on the
    'Nop.' prefix precisely so Payments.PayPalDirect's private PayPal SDK still deploys".
    That statement is TRUE and NOT SUFFICIENT, and it had never been exercised, because none
    of group 10's three plugins has a package runtime asset to deploy.

    MEASURED: with everything above in place and nothing else, the deployment directory
    contained Description.txt, logo.jpg, the plugin dll, its pdb and its deps.json - and NO
    PayPal.dll. Removing the filter target entirely did not change that (it only made
    Nop.Data.dll leak, reproducing section 83.3), so the filter is innocent.

    THE REAL CAUSE is an SDK default that has nothing to do with the filter:
    **$(CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies) is FALSE for a class library.** Only an application
    (Exe / Web) copies its NuGet packages' runtime assets to its output directory; a library
    copies only ProjectReference outputs, on the assumption that whoever consumes it will do
    the package resolution. A nopCommerce plugin breaks that assumption completely: it is
    deployed as a bare directory that PluginManager shadow-copies, nothing "consumes" it as a
    library, and its deps.json is never read - PluginManager loads assemblies by PATH into the
    default AssemblyLoadContext, which resolves their dependencies from the HOST's
    trusted-platform list. A dependency the host does not have must therefore sit physically
    next to the plugin, or it does not exist at runtime.

    WHY THIS WOULD HAVE FAILED SILENTLY, WHICH IS WHY IT IS WORTH THIS MUCH TEXT: the build is
    clean, the plugin is discovered, PluginManager records it as compatible, it appears in the
    admin plugin list and can be installed. The first thing that fails is
    PaypalHelper.GetApiContext -> new OAuthTokenCredential(...), i.e. the first real PayPal
    call - a FileNotFoundException on a money path, at runtime, in production.

    THE FIX, and why it is shaped this way. Turning $(CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies) on globally
    for the project was rejected: it would deploy the ENTIRE transitive package closure
    reached through Nop.Core / Nop.Services / Nop.Web.Framework - Autofac, Newtonsoft.Json,
    EF Core, ImageSharp, EPPlus, the Azure and IdentityModel stacks - every one of which the
    host already carries and each of which PluginManager would shadow-copy and load as the
    process's copy. That is the same failure mode section 83.3 documents for Nop.Data.dll,
    multiplied (measured: @(RuntimeCopyLocalItems) holds 50 items here, of which exactly ONE
    is PayPal's). Instead the target below copies exactly the assets of the ONE package that
    must travel with the plugin, selected by NuGetPackageId rather than by a hardcoded path,
    so the version pin in src/Directory.Packages.props stays the single source of truth.

    TWO EARLIER SHAPES OF THIS FIX WERE TRIED AND MEASURED TO FAIL, which is why it is a plain
    Copy rather than the more idiomatic ReferenceCopyLocalPaths contribution:
      * Adding the asset to @(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths) from a target hooked
        AfterTargets="ResolveReferences" or BeforeTargets="_CopyFilesMarkedCopyLocal". The item
        was verified present (a diagnostic Message showed ReferenceCopyLocalPaths going from 0
        to 1 entry, PayPal.dll) and the file was still NOT copied - _CopyFilesMarkedCopyLocal
        does not pick up a late contribution here. Note the "0" in that measurement: it also
        shows that NopPluginDoNotDeployHostAssemblies above empties the list completely rather
        than only its Nop.* entries, which is harmless given nothing else may be deployed, but
        is not what its name suggests.
      * Target-level Inputs/Outputs batching on @(RuntimeCopyLocalItems), which silently
        batched the whole target per item and made the guard below fire.

    The <Error> guard is deliberate: if a future version bump, retargeting or NuGet change
    stops resolving the asset, the build FAILS with an explanatory message instead of quietly
    producing a plugin that installs and then throws on its first payment.

    ASSERTED AT RUNTIME, not just built: Nop.Web.SmokeTests.PluginViewRenderTests checks that
    PayPal.dll is present in this plugin's deployment directory, that the four other payment
    plugins deploy NO third-party assembly at all, and that the types PayPal.dll needs from
    the System.Web and System.Configuration facades are loadable inside the running host.
  -->
  <Target Name="NopPluginDeployPayPalSdk" AfterTargets="Build">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_NopPayPalSdkAsset Include="@(RuntimeCopyLocalItems->WithMetadataValue('NuGetPackageId','PayPal'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error Condition="'@(_NopPayPalSdkAsset)' == ''"
           Text="The PayPal SDK runtime asset was not resolved. Payments.PayPalDirect cannot work without PayPal.dll next to it - see the long note in this project file. Check the PayPal PackageReference and its central version pin." />
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(_NopPayPalSdkAsset)"
          DestinationFiles="@(_NopPayPalSdkAsset->'$(OutDir)%(DestinationSubPath)')"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
    <ItemGroup>
      <FileWrites Include="@(_NopPayPalSdkAsset->'$(OutDir)%(DestinationSubPath)')" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Update="Views\**\*.cshtml"
             Link="Plugins\Payments.PayPalDirect\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="Never" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Include, NOT Update: under the Razor SDK .txt/.jpg arrive as <None>. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Remove="Description.txt" />
    <None Remove="logo.jpg" />
    <None Remove="Notes.txt" />
    <Content Include="Description.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="logo.jpg" CopyToOutputDirectory="Always" />
    <Content Include="Notes.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="Never" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
